@module stardust ; The stardust: 16 moving dots in the space views, and the in-system jump
@about Stardust $7898 runs once a pass in a space view (f1, w1, d1: 1248 runs), after ClearView has emptied the back buffer. The 16 dots of StarDots $7E8D (5 bytes each) move by the speed, the roll and the pitch; each dot's back buffer address and pixel mask go into the list StarList $7F82 (StarPlot), and when the last dot is done StarPlotAll $7C92 XORs every listed pixel into the buffer. Nothing is erased: ClearView has cleared the buffer, and the list is started again each pass. The front view moves the dots away from the centre, the rear view towards it, the side views sideways (StardustSide $7BFC). A dot off the screen is made again at random (StarRespawn $7D55).
@about The J key's in-system jump (InSystemJump $7EDE) moves the sun, the planet and the station $2000 closer, and makes the next stardust frame draw two passes at speeds $1E and $10 (StarWarp $7EDD).

$7898 c Stardust ; Move and draw the 16 dots for the view; after a J jump, two fast passes first
$78B1 c StardustPass ; One pass: the rates into StarRoll and StarPitch, the speed halved + 1, then every dot
> StarCount $7F7C counts the dots (16, or 4 in witch space: WitchSpace); a change of view (StarView $7F7B against ViewIndex) reshuffles the table (StarShuffle). Each dot's 5 bytes are unpacked into StarX, StarY (sign in bit 7, moved to StarSigns bits 7 and 6), StarZ, StarXFrac, StarYFrac; the roll and pitch signs go to StarSigns bits 4 and 3.
