@module linedraw ; Lines in the 3D view: clipping to the 256 x 128 window, the octant, and four patched Bresenham loops
@about A line's ends come as offsets from the view's centre with their quadrant bits (C: bits 2-3 the first end's, D E, bits 0-1 the second's, H L; bit 0 below the centre, bit 1 left of it: the signs of y and x, as ProjectedPoints keeps them), each offset 0-$7F across and 0-$3F down. DrawLine gives each end an outcode (bit 0 past x $80, bit 1 past y $40); both outside on the same side, nothing is drawn (the table LineSteps $AFF0); one or both outside, ClipLine moves the far end to the window's edge along the slope. LineOctant works out the slope as an 8-bit fraction (LineFraction) and the octant; LineSetup patches the step direction (RLC C or RRC C for left or right, INC L or DEC L at a byte edge) into one of four loops, named by the way they go and the axis that steps on the carry: LineUpX and LineDownX y major (a row a step, x on the carry), LineUpY and LineDownY x major. The line is drawn from D E towards H L. Each loop ORs (or XORs, UpdateShips's patch at $B435, $B465, $B486, $B4B2 while the sun is large) a pixel per step and adds the fraction to E, stepping the minor axis on the carry.

$B040 w ClipSave ; ClipLine's saved outcodes and slope
$B042 w ClipSlope
$B044 c DrawLine ; Draw a line between two points of the view (D E and H L, quadrants in C), clipped to the window
> 14814 runs. $FF in both coordinates of an end (a hidden point) draws nothing.
