$D8F8 c PrintPairChar ; A pair's character unless "?"
$D8FF b ControlData 3 ; Three bytes no code reads (known from the code only)
@module commander ; The commander menu (load and save on tape, the security number), the view names and the message line
@about CommanderMenu $D90A is the ACCESS MENU page (SYMBOL SHIFT docked, KeySteer bit 5 in FlightLoop, or Y on the title; other token 1): 1 loads a commander, 2 saves one, 3 leaves. A commander on tape is one headerless data block of the 102 bytes $D054-$D0B9, read and written by the ROM's LD-BYTES and SA-BYTES entered past their prologues. After a load or a save the record is also copied to DefaultCommander ($D232), the commander a new game (and the death) starts from. Neither tape path ran in the recordings (known from the code only).
@about The bottom of the space view is the message line (row 14 of the screen above the dashboard): MessageTick keeps the ten message slots ($D0BD-$D0D0), shows one for 14 passes, and between messages raises the periodic ones (ENERGY LOW, FUEL SCOOPS ON, DOCKING COMPUTERS ON, the mission's MAYDAY texts).

$D902 b MenuBands 5 ; The menu page's colour bands for ColourBands: one cell and a gap of 4 rows, two cells and 2 rows twice, two cells and 3 rows, sixteen cells and the end
$D907 b TapeLoaded ; 1 once a commander has been loaded: CommanderMenu returns it
$D908 w MenuStack ; SP at the menu's start: every pass and every error path starts again from it
$D90A c CommanderMenu ; The ACCESS MENU: 1 load, 2 save, 3 leave; returns A = 1 (NZ) when a commander was loaded
> The page is cleared, coloured (MenuBands, the message row, the top three attribute rows $0F and a rule at $4740 by MenuTop), and other token 1 prints the three choices. The keys are read by WaitKey; the stack is reset from MenuStack before each, so a BREAK or an error deep in the tape code just comes back here.
$D927 CommanderMenuKey
$D939 CommanderMenuExit
$D946 c LoadCommander ; 1: the name to look for (empty: the first commander found), then blocks until the names agree
> The ROM's LD-BYTES is entered at $0562, after its own INC D / EX AF,AF' / DEC D / DI (copied here before the CALL), so it returns to the game and not through SA/LD-RET. The record is loaded in place over $D054: a block whose name differs is simply followed by the next one. FOUND COMMANDER (other $D6) names each block read.
$D94C LoadCommanderBlock
$D965 LoadCommanderName
$D972 LoadCommanderCompare
$D980 c SaveCommander ; 2: the name into the record, the sun's place into its tail, START TAPE, SA-BYTES, then the security number
> SA-BYTES is entered at $04C6, past the PUSH of SA/LD-RET, so it comes back here with the border as it left it (set black at once) and the interrupts off (EI here). The nine bytes of SunPosition go into the record's tail $D0B1 so a loaded commander gets its sun back.
$D9AC c SecurityNumber ; Print "SECURITY NUMBER:" and two five-digit numbers made from the combat score, the galaxy, two mission flags and the cash
> D takes the galaxy (bits 0-2) and the mission flags' bits 3 and 6, three places up, then the parities of E (the combat score's middle byte, doubled), H and L (the cash in thousands): Parity shifts each in from the top. The four bytes are then chained: D = L xor D + $3D, E = D xor E + $66, H = E xor H + $8F, L = H xor L + $C7, and DE and HL are printed. The game never reads the number back; a checker with the same chain can test a claimed rank and cash (inference: the code only prints it).
$D9CB SecurityNumberIfZero
$D9D1 SecurityNumberGetCommanderRest3
$DA08 c KeepCommander ; The record becomes DefaultCommander (93 bytes), back to the menu
$DA13 CommanderMenuAgain
$DA16 c Parity ; The parity of A's eight bits into the top of D (C = the count of ones, its bit 0 shifted in)
$DA1A ParityLoop
$DA1E ParityNext
$DA25 c LoadFailed ; One frame, then BREAK if SPACE is held (FC0D = $20), else TAPE LOADING ERROR and the record back from DefaultCommander
$DA2D c TapeBreak ; The message lines cleared and BREAK
$DA37 c TapeError
$DA49 c TapeBlock ; IX = the record, DE = 102, A = $FF (a data block)
$DA53 c ReadName ; Read a name of up to 10 letters into HL after COMMANDER'S NAME? (other 8)
$DA5A c TapeMessage ; Other token A on the message row 22, after the row is cleared; a new line, a click
$DA72 b MessageTimer ; Counts 70 passes between the periodic messages
$DA73 b MessageShown ; The passes the message on the line has left (14 from MessageShow)
$DA74 b MessagePass ; Toggled each pass: the slots are scanned on even passes, the periodic messages on odd ones
$DA75 b MessageColumn ; The message's column on row 14
$DA76 b MessageToken ; The message's standard code: $FD (token $5D, " DESTROYED") after MessageItem, $E2 (token $42, " CR") after MessageNumber, 0 none
$DA77 w MessageNumber ; The bounty AddCash shows, in tenths
$DA79 b MessageItem ; The item DamageItem destroyed, a standard code (equipment $6C on, cargo $CC on)
$DA7A c ViewTexts ; The space view's name ("FRONT VIEW" and the like) into the back buffer, then MessageTick
> Called each pass (1220 times in c1). The name is plotted with SET 7 at $D50A, so PlotChar writes into the back buffer at $C000 and the frame copy shows it on top of the stars. On the screens (view 4 and up) the plot byte $D548 is set to write over (NOP) while MessageTick runs and back to mixing (XOR C) after.
$DA93 ViewTextsSpace
$DAB4 c MessageTick ; The jump countdown, the condition light, then the message line: show, scan the slots, or raise a periodic message
> The ten slots at $D0BD hold a column and a code each, bit 7 of the column a request (FullMessage and the market set them). On an even pass the first request found is taken (its bit cleared, a click) and shown for 14 passes. A shown message is plotted once a pass from its fourth pass on and only in the space views, so it blinks on as the screen copy overwrites it: XOR plotting twice would clear it.
$DAC9 MessageScan
$DACE MessageScanLoop
$DAD7 MessageTake
$DADC MessageTickJoin
$DAE1 c MessageShow ; Show standard code B at column C for 14 passes
$DAE6 c MessageSet ; Code B at column C, the count left as it is
$DAEF MessagePrint
$DB0D MessagePrintCode
$DB13 MessageDestroyed ; $FD: the item's name (three columns on, except code $73) and DESTROYED
$DB20 MessageDestroyedName
$DB25 MessageBounty ; $E2: the bounty and CR
$DB2E c MessagePeriodic ; Every 70 passes: ENERGY LOW, FUEL SCOOPS ON (and the fuel), DOCKING COMPUTERS ON, or the mission's MAYDAY
> In this order: the fourth energy bank below $9F gives ENERGY LOW (code $64) at once each odd pass and turns the cloak off (CloakOn 0); the sun near with a fuel scoop fitted adds 0.1 light years (SetFuel) and shows FUEL SCOOPS ON (code $A0); the docking computer on shows DOCKING COMPUTERS ON (code $7B). Otherwise MessageTimer counts 70 odd passes, then the mission state 1 shows its MAYDAY (code $0F, token $81: "... INVADED") and NovaMission (the second mission) "MAYDAY CORIOLIS IN DANGER" (code $C2) unless the sun is already growing.
$DB48 MessagePeriodicDue
$DB60 MessagePeriodicCount
$DB62 MessageInvaded
$DB67 MessageDocking
$DB6C MessageScooping
$DB7D MessageEnergyLow
$DB8E MessagePeriodicShow
$DB91 c ClearMessages ; Every slot's request bit cleared and the line's code to 0
$DB96 ClearMessagesLoop
$DBA1 b JumpCountdown ; The hyperspace countdown (twice the seconds shown); 0 when no jump is pending
@module hyperspace ; The jump keys, the countdown, the arrival in a new system or in witch space, the galactic jump, the three missions' triggers
@about H starts a hyperspace jump to the selected system, H with G held a galactic one (with the galactic hyperdrive). A countdown of 10 shows at the top left; at 0 JumpArrive makes the selected system the current one, places the sun, the planet and the station from its seeds, builds the market and runs the tunnel (TunnelLoop). Witch space comes instead with one chance in 256 (StarSeed $F9) or always with option bit 6 of the pause menu (F): the ship stops half way, with no sun, planet or station, among Thargoids (WitchSpace).
@about Outside galaxy 1 the jump count ($D063) starts the missions: at 32 jumps the sun goes nova (NovaMission, a FUEL LEAK), at 128 the cloak ship may come (MissionFlags bit 1), at 254 the Thargoid MAYDAY (MissionState 1). An arrival may also leave the station pirate-held (PirateStation). The recordings jumped twice (f1 to a system, w1 into witch space); the galactic jump and the missions are known from the code only.

$DBA2 c JumpKeys ; H or G+H (KeyViews $FC0A bits 4, 5): a jump, unless one counts down; docked: DOCKED and the status screen
> Called each pass through JumpKeysVector (197283 runs, 4 with a key). The keys are cleared at once. A hyperspace needs the distance to the selected system (FindNearest, JumpDistance) above 0, below 25.6 light years and within the fuel, else HYPERSPACE RANGE ? (code $C1); a galactic jump needs the galactic hyperdrive ($D08D). Then TunnelOn 1 (a jump) or 2 (galactic), the countdown 21 and HYPERSPACE -- <system> (code $C0) or GALACTIC HYPERSPACE (code $74) on the message line, a click, and the front view if a space view was on.
$DBBD JumpGalactic
$DBCF JumpHyper
$DBE3 JumpStart
$DBEB JumpCount
$DC04 JumpOutOfRange
$DC0A JumpDocked
$DC29 c JumpTick ; Each MessageTick: the countdown (shown halved, two digits at 1,1), and at 0 the jump itself
> 1220 runs in c1, f1 and w1; 42 counted, 2 jumped. The jump: the message slots cleared; a galactic jump (TunnelOn 2) or any jump out of the nova system goes to the next galaxy (GalaxyJump); a hyperspace takes the fuel (the distance), sets the fuel's refill price ($7060 = 70 - the fuel left + 2 x the economy), halves the legal status, and either misjumps (JumpWitch) or arrives (JumpArrive).
$DC32 JumpTickShow
$DC44 JumpNow
$DC4E c GalaxyJump ; The next galaxy: the missions reset (the nova over, the Thargoids forgotten), the hyperdrive used up, the seeds twisted, the legal status clean, the system nearest the cursor
> The fuel price $7060 gets the galaxy number (1-8) here. Each of the six seed bytes is rotated left one bit (TwistSeed) as BBC Elite does; the galaxy number wraps from 8 to 1.
$DC5F GalaxyJumpNext
$DC83 c TwistSeed ; The two bytes at HL rotated left one bit each, HL two on
$DC8E JumpHyperspace
$DCC0 c JumpWitch ; The misjump: WitchSpace on, the current system half way to the selected one, no safe zone, no system names
> The current system's coordinates ($D099, $D097) and the cursor become the mid points; the names of the current and the selected system are emptied ($DEE4, $DEED), so the status page shows none. w1: F of the pause menu (option bit 6) forced it.
$DCE9 c JumpArrive ; The arrival: the selected system becomes the current one; the sun, the planet and the station from its seeds; the market
> The seeds ($DEBA) go to the current system ($D096), the selected name to the current name, the tech level and the government to $D09C and $D09D, NewMarket with a fresh fluctuation byte, the sun and the planet cleared, StationLaunch $FF. The coordinates' top bytes come from the seeds: the sun's x and y (s1, s2 high bytes AND $83 OR 1: within ±3 pages, never 0) and z (s1 high OR low, AND 7, + 4, negative: behind), the planet's x and y the same way from s0 and s1's low bytes and z (s0 high OR low, AND 7, + 3) ahead. The station starts at the planet (slot 0 copied from PlanetPosition) and is moved about a page off along z or x (a seed bit picks; the middle byte's bit 7 set, the top byte one nearer or further), its axes set from the side it was moved to. f1: 1 run.
$DD18 JumpArriveLoop
$DD6D JumpArriveStation
$DD7D JumpArriveOffset
$DD92 JumpArriveNear
$DD93 JumpArriveStep
$DD96 JumpArriveFar
$DD97 JumpArriveSide
$DDA5 JumpArriveLoop2
$DDB7 JumpArriveX
$DDCD JumpArriveXFlip
$DDD0 JumpArriveZ
$DDE3 MissionStationGone ; The Thargoid base was destroyed (the station's role bit 7): MissionState 5, the award at the next dock
$DDE7 JumpArriveZPlus
$DDE9 JumpArriveAxes
$DDEB JumpArriveCursor
$DDFD c MissionTriggers ; Outside galaxy 1: the Thargoid mission's stage, the jump count, the three triggers, the pirate station's chance
> MissionState 1 becomes 2 (the MAYDAY left unanswered), 4 becomes 3 (left the base); arriving in the galaxy's first system (IsFirstSystem) with the mission on makes it 4. The jump count at 32 starts the nova (flag bit 0, NovaMission, the red tint, FuelLeak), at 128 the cloak ship (bit 1), at 254 the MAYDAY (bit 2, MissionState 1). With no trigger the station turns pirate-held when StarSeed is at most (7 - government) / 2: one chance in 256 for a corporate state, four for an anarchy.
$DE13 MissionTriggersLeft
$DE18 MissionTriggersBase
$DE22 MissionTriggersSetMissionState
$DE25 MissionTriggersCount
$DE39 c PirateChance ; The station pirate-held (its roll counter's bit 7 cleared) with a chance by the government
$DE4C c JumpEnd ; The tunnel next (TunnelLoop 1), the front view, the cabin temperature, SunNear, MissileState and MissionShip 0, the altitude $A0
$DE67 c MissionNova ; 32 jumps: the sun goes nova: flag bit 0, NovaMission, the view red, a fuel leak
$DE7E c MissionCloak ; 128 jumps: the cloak ship may come (flag bit 1)
$DE88 c MissionThargoids ; 254 jumps: the Thargoid MAYDAY (flag bit 2, MissionState 1)
$DE97 c FuelLeak ; All the fuel lost, FUEL LEAK on the message line (slot 9), when there was any
$DEA2 c SetFuel ; The fuel = A, at most 70 (7.0 light years); the fuel bar 2.25 x A
$DEA8 SetFuelStore
@module galaxy ; The galaxy's 256 systems from three seed words: the twist, the nearest system, the distance, the names, the system data and the market
@about Elite's galaxy is generated, never stored: a system is three 16-bit seeds (s0, s1, s2), and the next system's seeds are s1, s2 and s0 + s1 + s2 (Twist), four twists a system. The galaxy's first system has the galaxy's seeds ($D090); the next galaxy rotates each of the six bytes left one bit (GalaxyJump). From the seeds come the chart position (x = s1 high, y = s0 high), the economy, government, tech level, population and productivity (SystemData) and the name (SystemName: three or four letter pairs of StdPairs $FDB3 picked by s2's top bits), all as in BBC Elite (bbcelite.com's deep dives agree with every step here).
@about The selected system (the cursor's, $DEBA) and the current one ($D096) are kept as seeds; the work copy at $DEC0 is the one twisted. A chart cursor move, a jump and a planet's name typed on the chart all end in FindNearest or FindSystem, which leave the selected system's data, name and distance here.

$DEB9 b GalaxySpare ; One byte no code reads (known from the code only)
$DEBA b SelectedSeeds 6 ; The selected system's seeds s0, s1, s2 (SystemData)
$DEC0 b SystemSeeds 6 ; The seeds being twisted: the system the loops are at
$DEC6 b BestSeeds 6 ; FindNearest's best system so far; FindSystem's place in the walk
$DECC b TextSeeds 4 ; The four bytes TextRandom shifts for the planet's description (from s1 and s2)
$DED0 t NameInput 11 ; The name typed: the commander's (JAMESON) or a planet's (FindSystem), zero-ended
$DEDB t NameBuffer 9 ; The name SystemName builds (up to eight letters, zero-ended)
$DEE4 t CurrentName 9 ; The current system's name (LAVE)
$DEED t SelectedName 9 ; The selected system's name
$DEF6 w JumpRange ; The distance to the selected system in 0.1 light years (FindNearest, JumpDistance)
$DEF8 b Economy ; The selected system's economy, 0-7 (0 rich industrial, 7 poor agricultural)
$DEF9 b Government ; Its government, 0-7 (0 anarchy, 7 corporate state)
$DEFA b TechLevel ; Its tech level less one, 0-14
$DEFB b Population ; Its population in 100 millions
$DEFC w Productivity ; Its productivity in millions of credits
$DEFE b SystemSpare 2 ; Two bytes no code reads (known from the code only)
$DF00 b SystemCount ; FindSystem's count of the systems tried (256 then not found)
$DF01 w NamePointer ; SystemName's next letter
$DF03 c NextGalaxy ; Screen 16 of ScreenTable, which nothing asks for: the galaxy number one more (wrapping at 8) and the galaxy's seeds twisted (unreachable; GalaxyJump does the same in the game)
$DF1A c TwistWord ; The two bytes at HL rotated left one bit each
$DF24 c FindSystem ; The system named in NameInput: walk the galaxy's 256 systems comparing NameBuffer; carry when none
> Found: the system becomes the selected one, the chart's cross is moved (EraseCross before and after SystemData), the name kept (KeepName) and the distance worked out (JumpDistanceNow). Called from the charts' PLANET NAME? prompt (PlanetNamePrompt), known from the code only.
$DF2B FindSystemNext
$DF37 FindSystemCompare
$DF43 FindSystemMiss
$DF4C FindSystemFound
$DF5E w BestDistance ; FindNearest's smallest squared distance so far
$DF60 c FindNearest ; The system nearest the chart cursor becomes the selected one, with the cross redrawn on the charts
$DF74 c FindNearestNow ; Walk the 256 systems for the smallest dx^2 + (dy/2)^2 to the cursor ($D0BB, $D0BC), then its data, name and distance
> 10 runs in f1 and d1 (a jump target and the charts). The y difference is halved because the charts are drawn half as tall as wide; the root of the best sum times 4 is the distance in 0.1 light years (JumpDistance), as the BBC's.
$DF7F FindNearestLoop
$DF9C FindNearestStep
$DFB5 JumpDistanceNow
$DFC4 c NextSystem ; Four twists: the next system's seeds
$DFC6 NextSystemLoop
$DFCE c SquaredDistance ; HL = (the cursor's x - B)^2 + ((the cursor's y - A) / 2)^2, B and A the system's x and y
$DFEA c AbsDifference ; A = |A - B|
$DFF0 AbsDifferenceStore
$DFF2 c JumpDistance ; JumpRange = 4 x the root of HL ($FFFF on a carry)
$DFF6 JumpDistanceFar
$DFF9 JumpDistanceRoot
$E005 JumpDistanceStore
$E009 c MultiplyDA ; HL = D x A (shift and add, eight steps)
$E010 MultiplyDALoop
$E019 MultiplyDANext
$E01C c SystemName ; The name of the system in SystemSeeds into NameBuffer: four pairs when bit 6 of s0's low byte is set, else three
$E024 SystemNameLoop
$E03D c NamePair ; One pair: s2's high byte AND $1F picks StdPairs entry (0: none), a "?" second letter left out; then a twist
$E060 NamePairNext
$E063 c Twist ; s0, s1, s2 = s1, s2, s0 + s1 + s2
$E080 c KeepName ; NameBuffer becomes SelectedName
$E08C c SystemData ; The selected system from SystemSeeds: its seeds, the cursor on it, the economy, government, tech level, population and productivity
> As BBC Elite: the government is s1's bits 3-5, the economy s0's high bits 0-2 (bit 1 set for governments 0 and 1); the tech level (7 - economy) + (s1 high AND 3) + government / 2 rounded up; the population 4 x tech + economy + government + 1; the productivity ((7 - economy) + 3) x (government + 4) x population x 8. 10 runs.
$E0BD SystemDataTech
$E107 c PrintDistance ; DISTANCE: and the jump range with its point and LIGHT YEARS, then a new line (nothing at 0)
$E123 PrintDistanceEnd
$E126 c GalaxySeeds ; SystemSeeds = the galaxy's seeds: the walk starts at its first system
$E12E c SaveSeeds ; BestSeeds = SystemSeeds
$E136 c RestoreSeeds ; SystemSeeds = BestSeeds
$E13C c CopySeeds ; Six bytes from HL to DE
$E142 c NewMarket ; A new fluctuation byte ($D06A) from StarSeed, then MakeMarket
$E148 c MakeMarket ; Each commodity's price ($E1BD) and the station's stock ($D09E) from Commodities, the economy and the fluctuation
> For each of the 17 entries (base price, factor, base quantity, mask): the product = economy x (factor AND $1F); the stock = base quantity + (fluctuation AND mask) less the product and one for a positive factor (INC A / NEG), plus the product for a negative one, 0 when negative, AND $3F; the price = (base price + (fluctuation AND mask) plus or minus the product) x 4, in tenths of a credit. The last commodity's stock (alien items) is forced to 0. 4 runs (d1 and the arrivals); tools/galaxy.py --market reproduces Diso's 17 prices and stocks of f1 exactly. NewGame turns the stock's store ($E1AE, LD (HL),A) into a NOP for its call, so the first market keeps the stock of the commander record.
$E154 MakeMarketLoop
$E164 MakeMarketLoop2
$E167 MakeMarketQuantity
$E17B MakeMarketStock
$E181 MakeMarketStockStore
$E19A MakeMarketPositive
$E19E MakeMarketPrice
$E1BB b PriceBase 2 ; Two bytes before Prices: the market screens' pointers start here and step 2 first
$E1BD w Prices 8 ; The 17 commodities' prices in tenths of a credit (MakeMarket)
$E1DF b MarketItem ; The market screens' row: the commodity (0-16), which also picks the unit (t, kg, g)
$E1E0 w HoldPointer ; The trading pages' pointer into the hold ($D06B on), stepped first
$E1E2 w PricePointer ; Their pointer into Prices
$E1E4 w StockPointer ; Their pointer into MarketStock (buying) or the hold, stepped first
$E1E6 w SavedCursor ; The text position SaveCursor keeps
@module menuhelpers ; The screens' helpers: the click, the key waits, the cursor moves, the market rows, the units, the line editor and the planet name prompt
@about The docked screens are printed line by line with these: NewLine and SetRow keep the text position in range (row 24 wraps to row 1), ColumnInline sets the column from the byte after its CALL, PrintUnit adds t, kg, g or km, and the market rows step three pointers through Prices, MarketStock and the hold. ReadLine is the one line editor (the commander's name, a planet's name): digits and capitals, DELETE (CAPS SHIFT + 0) and ENTER.

$E1E8 c Click ; With the sound on (Options bit 7) a short beep through the ROM's BEEPER (HL = $80, DE = $60)
$E1F7 c WaitKey ; 20 frames, the key bytes cleared, then wait for a key: A = its code (KeyCode $FC0D)
$E1FD WaitKeyLoop
$E205 c ClearKeys4 ; The four key bytes $FC08-$FC0B zeroed with the interrupts off
$E20B ClearKeys4Loop
$E212 c MarketRow ; One commodity of the market: its name, the unit, the price (4 digits and the point), the stock and its unit; carry when the stock is 0 ("-")
> MarketItem picks the name (standard code $D0 + item); the columns are 13, 15 and 23 (ColumnInline). The price comes through PricePointer, the stock through StockPointer, each stepped first.
